Saunadust

Ingredients: BOSSA NOVA + TRIP HOP + CELESTIAL, SPACE & ORBITAL + Bass Guitar
Creative Catalyst: Born among nomadic salt-flat traders who barter rescued Japanese analog synthesizers, rigging circuit boards to run on solar batteries beneath unclouded starfields.

Saunadust is the sound of sultry, seaside Brazilian sophistication dragged across dry, alkaline plains and pointed toward the exosphere. The genre marries the intricate, syncopated nylon-string comping and intimate vocal whispers of bossa nova with the heavy, downtempo swing and vinyl crackle of Bristol trip hop. Deep, rounded bass guitar notes anchor the groove, while rescued analog synthesizers drift overhead like slow-moving communication satellites, casting long harmonic trails across wide-open stereo panoramas.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 78–88 BPM. A languid, swung downtempo groove where the crisp, syncopated rim-click of bossa nova clashes seamlessly with the weighted, dragging kick-and-snare pocket of trip hop.
- Harmonic Palette: Major 7th, minor 9th, and flat-fifth altered chords voiced on warm nylon strings, counterbalanced by microtonal analog synthesizer drones and lydian-mode cosmic pads.
- Production Signatures: 
  - Tape-Baked Orbital Delays: Long, self-oscillating tape delay throws filtered to emulate the cold, decaying telemetry of deep-space transmissions.
  - Dry-vs-Infinite Staging: Hyper-dry, close-mic’d vocal breath and fingers wiping across flatwound bass strings, juxtaposed against cavernous, shimmering plate reverbs on synth leads.
  - Solar Drift Flutter: Pitch-warble and subtle voltage sagging introduced by running rescued circuitry through unstable solar-battery rigs.
- Dynamics: Songs open as whispered, skeletal lounge sketches before sub-bass weight drops in; tracks breathe through extended celestial mid-sections where chords dissolve into starlight, eventually pulling back to a stark, singular acoustic rhythm.

Scene & Origin Lore
The sound materialized along the desolate trade roads of the Atacama basin, where traveling merchants haul refurbished 1980s synthesizers between isolated lithium towns. At dusk, the caravanners park their flatbeds in circles, firing up modified soldering irons and patch bays powered by solar collectors. Dressed in loose-woven vicuña ponchos, grease-stained flight jackets, and mirrored anti-glare goggles, the musicians began laying Brazilian nylon-string chord progressions over crunchy MPC beats recorded to decaying cassettes. The basslines—plucked by thumb on battered hollow-body electric basses—ground the music while the repaired keyboards send pristine, extraterrestrial arpeggios upward into the driest, clearest night sky on Earth.

Typical Instruments: Flatwound-string electric bass guitar, classical nylon-string guitar, rescued analog synthesizers (Roland Juno-60, Korg MS-20), Akai MPC drum samplers, brushed snare, and whispered Portuguese-Spanish bilingual vocals.

For Fans Of: Portishead, Stan Getz, Floating Points, Cibelle

