Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: The Lichen Calibrates
- Story & Context: When Mateo first bypassed the burned-out power supply on an imported Korg synthesizer at the oasis outpost of San Pedro, a tiny clump of dried lichen tumbled from the circuit board just as the speakers produced a pristine, warm sine tone. The caravanners ceased their trading to listen to the machine hum against a sluggish, dragging kick drum and a bossa nova nylon strum, prompting Alana to lean over the microphone and murmur the first words of the movement: "We root in the gravel, but we bloom in the dark." The track marks the precise afternoon the trade route stopped dealing exclusively in hardware and began pressing their own limited vinyl run.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: An extreme close-up of a vintage circuit board covered in dust and dried green desert lichen, tiny orange LEDs glowing through cracked glass, illuminated by the low golden sun of a desert horizon, stylized as an indie 1970s album cover.

Track 2: Estufa de Vidro
- Story & Context: Written during a three-day sandstorm while Alana and Mateo were trapped inside an experimental agricultural glasshouse, the track captures the claustrophobia of condensation dripping onto an electric bass guitar. Mateo plucked the muted bassline with a rag shoved under the bridge to deaden the sustain while Alana recorded the glass rattling against the howling wind, whispering into an old ribbon mic: "Tell me how the vine climbs when nobody holds the glass." The confession exposes the strain of years spent drifting along trading roads, trading love for spare chips and clean electricity.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Looking through a steamy, condensation-streaked greenhouse window at night, lush tropical ferns pressed against the glass from inside, while outside a vast expanse of barren desert sand sweeps away beneath an enormous, crystal-clear starry sky.

Track 3: What the Seedling Heard in Orbit
- Story & Context: Local myth claims that an abandoned satellite relay station south of the Bolivian border still captures radio echoes of this track, which was recorded using an antenna array that fed deep-space telemetry back into an analog delay pedal. During the tracking session, a stray gust blew the caravan's seed-tray off the roof, scattering heirloom tomato seeds into the cooling cooling-fins of an idling generator. As the nylon strings faded beneath a monstrous, sub-heavy trip-hop descent, Alana sang the outro into the night breeze: "Scatter me backward into the cold air."
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A solitary heirloom seed floating weightlessly in deep space, glowing faintly with bioluminescent veins, framed against the soft blue curvature of the Earth and the distant golden glow of desert caravans below.

